<!-- MIDDLE: ARTICLE -->
<article class="post-body">

<!-- ─────────────── §01 ─────────────── -->
<section id="s-intro" data-code="intro">
<div class="section-no">01 — States, Not Tokens</div>
<h2>The same token, two different updates</h2>
<p>An autoregressive policy <span style="font-family:var(--ff-mono)">π<sub>θ</sub>(y<sub>t</sub> | x, y<sub>&lt;t</sub>)</span> always predicts the next token <em>conditioned on a state</em> — the prompt <span style="font-family:var(--ff-mono)">x</span> plus everything generated so far <span style="font-family:var(--ff-mono)">y<sub>&lt;t</sub></span>. The paper's central move is to stop staring at the loss function and start asking: <strong>which states does the supervision actually land on?</strong></p>
<p>Why does that matter? Predicting the number <code>72</code> after a <em>correct</em> chain of reasoning and predicting <code>72</code> after an <em>inconsistent</em> chain are different policy updates — they touch different conditioning contexts. The diagram below shows a GSM8K problem. The top row is the gold dataset trajectory SFT trains on. The bottom row is where the model actually <em>is</em> after it makes its own early mistake — and SFT never trained it to recover from there.</p>

<div class="vp">
  <div class="vp-head">
    <span class="vp-title">trajectory.gsm8k — gold states vs. the model's own states</span>
    <span class="vp-hint">toggle</span>
  </div>
  <div class="vp-body">
    <div class="traj-block">
      <div class="traj-row" id="traj-gold">
        <div class="traj-tag gold">Gold trajectory (what SFT supervises)</div>
        <div class="traj-states">
          <div class="traj-state">Q: Natalia sold 48 clips…</div>
          <div class="traj-arrow">→</div>
          <div class="traj-state ok">half as many: 48/2 = 24</div>
          <div class="traj-arrow">→</div>
          <div class="traj-state ok">total: 48 + 24 = 72</div>
          <div class="traj-arrow">→</div>
          <div class="traj-state ok">#### 72 ✓</div>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="traj-row" id="traj-roll">
        <div class="traj-tag roll">Model rollout (where it actually lives)</div>
        <div class="traj-states">
          <div class="traj-state">Q: Natalia sold 48 clips…</div>
          <div class="traj-arrow">→</div>
          <div class="traj-state bad">twice as many: 48×2 = 96</div>
          <div class="traj-arrow">→</div>
          <div class="traj-state bad">total: 96 + 48 = 144</div>
          <div class="traj-arrow">→</div>
          <div class="traj-state fail">#### 144 ✗</div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="canvas-controls">
      <button class="canvas-btn active" id="traj-btn">Highlight where the model actually is</button>
    </div>
    <div class="canvas-caption" id="traj-cap">
      Both rows start from the <b>same prompt</b>, but after the model's own error (<b>48×2</b> instead of <b>48/2</b>) it occupies states the gold data never contains. SFT pours dense supervision on the top row — yet at inference the model lives on the bottom row. This is <b>exposure bias recast as a state-distribution mismatch</b>.
    </div>
  </div>
</div>

<p>This is the thesis in one image: post-training is not only about what signal you apply (a label, a reward, a teacher logit) — it is about the <strong>state distribution</strong> that signal is applied on. Get the states wrong and even a perfectly dense, correct label can drag the policy somewhere it can't recover from.</p>
</section>

<!-- ─────────────── §02 ─────────────── -->
<section id="s-axes" data-code="axes">
<div class="section-no">02 — Two Axes</div>
<h2>State source × signal source</h2>
<p>Every post-training method is really two independent choices that the algorithm name usually bundles together:</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>State source</strong> — where do the contexts <span style="font-family:var(--ff-mono)">s</span> come from? Dataset, teacher rollouts, or the student's own rollouts?</li>
  <li><strong>Signal source</strong> — what target is used at those contexts? A gold token, a teacher distribution, or a reward?</li>
</ul>
<p>Click a method to see where it sits. The thing to watch: methods that land in <span style="color:var(--green)">green-bordered</span> cells draw their states from the <strong>learner itself</strong> — they are <em>on-policy</em>. OPD and offline KD use the <em>same</em> teacher signal, but live in completely different rows.</p>

<div class="vp">
  <div class="vp-head">
    <span class="vp-title">decompose.py — the two axes of post-training</span>
    <span class="vp-hint">click a method</span>
  </div>
  <div class="vp-body">
    <table class="axes-grid">
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th class="rowhead">state ↓ &nbsp; signal →</th>
          <th>Gold token</th>
          <th>Teacher logits / continuations</th>
          <th>Reward</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <th class="rowhead">Dataset</th>
          <td data-method="sft"><span class="cellname">SFT</span></td>
          <td>—</td>
          <td>—</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <th class="rowhead">Teacher rollouts</th>
          <td>—</td>
          <td data-method="kd"><span class="cellname">Offline KD</span></td>
          <td>—</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <th class="rowhead">Student rollouts</th>
          <td class="onpol" data-method="dagger"><span class="cellname">DAgger</span></td>
          <td class="onpol" data-method="opd"><span class="cellname">OPD</span></td>
          <td>—</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <th class="rowhead">Current policy</th>
          <td>—</td>
          <td>—</td>
          <td class="onpol" data-method="rl"><span class="cellname">RL</span></td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
    <div class="method-btns">
      <button class="canvas-btn active" data-m="sft">SFT</button>
      <button class="canvas-btn" data-m="kd">Offline KD</button>
      <button class="canvas-btn" data-m="opd">OPD</button>
      <button class="canvas-btn" data-m="rl">RL</button>
      <button class="canvas-btn" data-m="dagger">DAgger</button>
    </div>
    <div class="legend-row">
      <span><span class="dot-g"></span> green border = states come from the learner (on-policy)</span>
    </div>
    <div class="canvas-caption" id="axes-cap"></div>
  </div>
</div>

<p>The punchline of the grid: <strong>OPD is closer to RL than to offline distillation</strong>, even though its loss is written as a supervised teacher–student objective. The signal is the teacher; the <em>states</em> are the student's. That mismatch between how a method is named and where it actually operates is exactly what the loss-function view hides.</p>
</section>

<!-- ─────────────── §03 ─────────────── -->
<section id="s-statespace" data-code="statespace">
<div class="section-no">03 — Policy State Space</div>
<h2>Where does the gradient land?</h2>
<p>Here is the whole paper in one picture. The soft green cloud is the <strong>model's reachable region</strong> — the states its own rollouts actually visit. Each method drops supervision markers somewhere in this space. The question that decides everything: <em>do the updates land inside the cloud, or out in territory the model can never recover from?</em></p>
<p>Flip between the three regimes and watch where the markers fall.</p>

<div class="vp">
  <div class="vp-head">
    <span class="vp-title">state_space.viz — on-policy vs. off-policy supervision</span>
    <span class="vp-hint">switch the regime</span>
  </div>
  <div class="vp-body">
    <canvas id="ss-canvas" class="viz-canvas" height="340"></canvas>
    <div class="canvas-controls">
      <button class="canvas-btn active" data-ss="sft">SFT (off-policy)</button>
      <button class="canvas-btn" data-ss="rl">RL (on-policy)</button>
      <button class="canvas-btn" data-ss="opd">OPD (student states, teacher signal)</button>
    </div>
    <div class="canvas-caption" id="ss-cap"></div>
  </div>
</div>

<div class="math-block">
<div class="math-label">one post-training step</div>
<span class="math-var">s</span> ~ q<sub>k</sub>(s)<span class="math-comment">      &nbsp;# 1. draw a training state</span><br>
<span class="math-var">z</span> ~ S(s)<span class="math-comment">         &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;# 2. signal provider gives a target</span><br>
θ<sub>k+1</sub> = θ<sub>k</sub> − η ∇<sub>θ</sub> ℓ(π<sub>θ</sub>(·|s), <span class="math-var">z</span>)<span class="math-comment">   # 3. update</span><br>
<br>
<span class="math-comment"># SFT:  q = d_data      (fixed, learner-independent)</span><br>
<span class="math-comment"># RL:   q = d^π_θ       (the learner's own rollouts)</span><br>
<span class="math-comment"># OPD:  q = d^π_student (student rollouts, signal from teacher)</span>
</div>

<p>SFT's <span style="font-family:var(--ff-mono)">q</span> doesn't depend on the current learner at all — so under pressure it can shove the policy toward red states that sit <em>outside</em> the cloud. RL and OPD draw <span style="font-family:var(--ff-mono)">q</span> from the learner, so every update is a <strong>local repair</strong> on a state the model can actually reach.</p>
</section>

<!-- ─────────────── §04 ─────────────── -->
<section id="s-sft" data-code="sft">
<div class="section-no">04 — SFT: Gentle or Destructive</div>
<h2>The same algorithm, two outcomes</h2>
<p>SFT is not inherently destructive. The deciding factor is <strong>training pressure</strong> — epochs, learning rate, and adapter rank. Low pressure keeps the dataset states compatible with the base policy. High pressure repeatedly forces the model toward narrow off-policy prefixes until it drifts away from general behavior — and, strikingly, even hurts the target task. Toggle the knob:</p>

<div class="vp">
  <div class="vp-head">
    <span class="vp-title">sft_pressure.run — Qwen3-0.6B-Base on GSM8K</span>
    <span class="vp-hint">turn the pressure up</span>
  </div>
  <div class="vp-body">
    <div class="canvas-controls" style="margin-top:0;margin-bottom:.5rem">
      <span class="canvas-label">training pressure:</span>
      <button class="canvas-btn active" data-sft="mild">Mild — 1 epoch, lr 2e-5</button>
      <button class="canvas-btn" data-sft="stress">Stress — 5 epochs, lr 5e-4, rank 64</button>
    </div>
    <div class="stat-pills" id="sft-pills"></div>
    <div class="twin-bars" id="sft-bars">
      <div class="tb-row">
        <div class="tb-label">GSM8K (target ↑)</div>
        <div class="tb-track"><div class="tb-fill" id="sft-bar-gsm" style="background:var(--cyan)"><span></span></div></div>
      </div>
      <div class="tb-row">
        <div class="tb-label">Retention ratio (↑)</div>
        <div class="tb-track"><div class="tb-fill" id="sft-bar-ret" style="background:var(--green)"><span></span></div></div>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="canvas-caption" id="sft-cap"></div>
  </div>
</div>

<p>Mild SFT lifts GSM8K from <strong>0.448 → 0.512</strong> with essentially no forgetting (retention ratio 1.0008 — it actually retains slightly <em>more</em>). Stress SFT collapses retention to <strong>0.826</strong> <em>and</em> drops GSM8K to 0.420, <em>below the base model</em>. Catastrophic forgetting here is not caused by maximum likelihood itself — it is caused by dense off-policy updates pushing the policy into regions that interact badly with its own future states.</p>
</section>

<!-- ─────────────── §05 ─────────────── -->
<section id="s-opd" data-code="opd">
<div class="section-no">05 — Beating the Teacher</div>
<h2>How a student surpasses its own teacher</h2>
<p>Take the broken stress-SFT model — GSM8K 0.420, TruthfulQA 0.245, MMLU 0.364 — and use it as the <em>only</em> supervision source for an on-policy distillation student. The student beats it on <strong>every</strong> task. How is that possible when the teacher is the only signal?</p>
<p>Because a teacher's measured score depends on two things: its local conditional distributions <em>and</em> the trajectories it tends to visit. OPD queries only the first. The student samples its own states, then asks the teacher: <em>"given where I am, what comes next?"</em> — never <em>"which states would you have visited instead of me?"</em> The teacher's bad global trajectory is never inherited.</p>

<div class="vp">
  <div class="vp-head">
    <span class="vp-title">opd.viz — query the teacher locally, don't copy its path</span>
    <span class="vp-hint">animated</span>
  </div>
  <div class="vp-body">
    <canvas id="opd-canvas" class="viz-canvas" height="320"></canvas>
    <div class="canvas-controls">
      <button class="canvas-btn" id="opd-play">▶ Replay</button>
      <span class="canvas-label">red = teacher's degraded full trajectory (not copied) · green = student path with local teacher repairs</span>
    </div>
    <div class="canvas-caption">
      The teacher's own rollout (<b>red</b>) wanders into poor states — that's why its global score is low. The student (<b>green</b>) walks its <i>own</i> path and at each step pulls a short <b>cyan</b> continuation from the teacher as a local repair. It harvests the teacher's useful local knowledge while sidestepping the trajectory distribution that made the teacher bad.
    </div>
  </div>
</div>

<div class="stat-pills">
  <div class="stat-pill"><div class="pv">0.420 → 0.466</div><div class="pl">GSM8K (teacher → student)</div></div>
  <div class="stat-pill up"><div class="pv">0.245 → 0.275</div><div class="pl">TruthfulQA</div></div>
  <div class="stat-pill up"><div class="pv">0.364 → 0.430</div><div class="pl">MMLU</div></div>
</div>

<div class="callout">
  <div class="ct">Practical recipe</div>
  <p>One-step next-token KL was too weak — it collapsed to GSM8K <strong>0.040</strong> in their runs. What worked was <strong>continuation-based</strong> OPD: the teacher generates short rollouts from student states and the student learns them with cross-entropy. Dense, trajectory-level signal + on-policy state source. This is DAgger for LLMs.</p>
</div>
</section>

<!-- ─────────────── §06 ─────────────── -->
<section id="s-drift" data-code="drift">
<div class="section-no">06 — Drift Isn't the Story</div>
<h2>Equal drift, wildly different forgetting</h2>
<p>The natural hypothesis: forgetting is just a function of how far the model's rollouts moved, so a scalar drift metric (here, rollout-state MMD with an RBF kernel) should predict it. The data say no. Stress SFT, OPD-from-stress, and RL all produce <strong>nearly identical MMD drift</strong> — yet their forgetting differs by an order of magnitude.</p>

<div class="vp">
  <div class="vp-head">
    <span class="vp-title">drift_vs_forgetting.chart — MMD can't explain the gap</span>
    <span class="vp-hint">scroll to animate</span>
  </div>
  <div class="vp-body">
    <h3 style="margin-top:0">MMD drift (≈ identical)</h3>
    <div class="twin-bars" id="drift-mmd">
      <div class="tb-row"><div class="tb-label">Stress SFT</div><div class="tb-track"><div class="tb-fill" data-w="99" style="background:rgba(107,114,128,.65)"><span>0.01093</span></div></div></div>
      <div class="tb-row"><div class="tb-label">OPD (stress teacher)</div><div class="tb-track"><div class="tb-fill" data-w="99" style="background:rgba(107,114,128,.65)"><span>0.01092</span></div></div></div>
      <div class="tb-row"><div class="tb-label">On-policy RL</div><div class="tb-track"><div class="tb-fill" data-w="99" style="background:rgba(107,114,128,.65)"><span>0.01098</span></div></div></div>
    </div>
    <h3>Forgetting (wildly different)</h3>
    <div class="twin-bars" id="drift-forget">
      <div class="tb-row"><div class="tb-label">Stress SFT</div><div class="tb-track"><div class="tb-fill" data-w="100" style="background:#f87171"><span>0.0635</span></div></div></div>
      <div class="tb-row"><div class="tb-label">OPD (stress teacher)</div><div class="tb-track"><div class="tb-fill" data-w="24" style="background:var(--amber)"><span>0.0155</span></div></div></div>
      <div class="tb-row"><div class="tb-label">On-policy RL</div><div class="tb-track"><div class="tb-fill" data-w="3" style="background:var(--green)"><span>0.0020</span></div></div></div>
    </div>
    <div class="canvas-caption">
      Same distance moved, <b>32× difference in forgetting</b>. The missing variable isn't <i>how far</i> the rollouts moved — it's <b>which states received supervision</b> and whether they were locally recoverable for the learner. Scalar drift must be read alongside the state source, not instead of it.
    </div>
  </div>
</div>
</section>

<!-- ─────────────── §07 ─────────────── -->
<section id="s-results" data-code="results">
<div class="section-no">07 — Results</div>
<h2>The full picture</h2>
<p>All runs use <strong>Qwen3-0.6B-Base</strong> with LoRA adapters on a single RTX 3090. GSM8K is the target; TruthfulQA and MMLU measure retention. Base scores: GSM8K 0.448, TruthfulQA 0.300, MMLU 0.436.</p>

<div class="results-bars" id="result-bars">
  <div class="bar-row"><div class="bar-label">Base</div><div class="bar-track"><div class="bar-fill" data-w="44.8" style="background:rgba(107,114,128,.55)"><span>0.448</span></div></div></div>
  <div class="bar-row"><div class="bar-label">Mild SFT</div><div class="bar-track"><div class="bar-fill" data-w="51.2" style="background:var(--cyan)"><span>0.512</span></div></div></div>
